---
/*
  /admin/notes — the pile (14 · Piece 1, plan §4; 10-hq §10c's "its own room").

  THE ROOM SHOWS THE WORDS. Every card holds the dump's own text — no title, no
  slug, no type mark, no checkbox, no open action. That is the whole point of
  this room existing, and it is a correction rather than an addition. Michael,
  2026-08-03, on the fragment manager's old `?view=notes`:

    "I have a notes fragment, and it's an uncomfortable middle ground because I
     have to title these things… I see untitled, untitled, untitled, and I have
     to open each one to see what I jotted down, unless I put a title, which I'm
     not going to do in a brain dump. All I care about is that I have writing
     fragments and I have brain dumps."

  So there are exactly two things now, and the third was deleted with this
  commit: `?view=notes`, the Notes button on the manager, and notes in the
  trash. The manager holds drafts and published; dumps live here.

  ⚠ THE STORAGE DID NOT CHANGE, and that was the deciding argument (14 §3). A
  dump is still a `writing` fragment at `status: 'note'`, so **make it a piece**
  is one status flip on the row you already typed — same id, same text, same
  history, no copy and no second write path. A `captures` table of its own would
  have bought an honest model (no slug, no title, ever) and paid for it with a
  migration and a cross-table move on the one motion that matters most.

  NO BOX AT THE TOP OF THIS PAGE. Writing is the ✚'s job, from every room
  including this one (CaptureDialog.astro). A box here as well would be a second
  place to type, and "which one do I use" is the middle ground arriving again in
  a different costume.
*/
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import AdminLayout from '../../layouts/AdminLayout.astro';
import AltTextDialog from '../../components/admin/AltTextDialog.astro';
import EditorToolbar from '../../components/admin/EditorToolbar.astro';
import EventSheet from '../../components/admin/EventSheet.astro';
import LinkDialog from '../../components/admin/LinkDialog.astro';
import LogSheet from '../../components/admin/LogSheet.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/admin/PageHeader.astro';
import TaskSheet from '../../components/admin/TaskSheet.astro';
import { elapsedSince } from '../../lib/hq/dates';
import { homeTimezone, localToday } from '../../lib/hq/time';
import { renderMarkdown } from '../../lib/markdown';

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;

// Newest first, by when it was last TOUCHED rather than created: editing a
// thought is thinking about it again, and it belongs back at the top.
//
// The cap is a backstop, not paging. The pile is meant to be triaged, so a
// healthy one is short; if it is ever genuinely 100 deep the answer is a search
// field, which is the moment to add one and not before.
const { data: rows } = await supabase
  .from('fragments')
  .select('id, body, slug, updated_at')
  .eq('status', 'note')
  .is('deleted_at', null)
  .order('updated_at', { ascending: false })
  .limit(100);

const notes = (rows ?? []).map((n) => ({
  id: n.id,
  body: n.body ?? '',
  /*
    ⚠ RENDERED, NOT PRINTED — since 2026-08-06 the pile's editor is TipTap, so
    a dump's body is genuinely Markdown and the card has to show what it means
    rather than how it is spelled.

    `breaks: true` is what makes that safe for everything already in the
    database. Every dump written before this change is plain text whose
    newlines are its shape; with breaks off they would collapse into one
    paragraph and the whole pile would silently reflow. Both spellings land in
    the same place — see the `renderMarkdown` tests, which pin exactly that.
  */
  html: renderMarkdown(n.body, { breaks: true }),
  // Both ride the card so an edit needs no round trip to start: the slug so
  // `saveWriting` doesn't re-derive (and churn) it, the token so a stale tab
  // gets a CONFLICT instead of overwriting a newer edit.
  slug: n.slug,
  updatedAt: n.updated_at,
  ago: elapsedSince(n.updated_at),
  // Roughly a card's worth. The clamp is CSS (10 lines); this only decides
  // whether the `more` control is offered, and it counts wrapped-looking length
  // as well as newlines so one long paragraph earns it too.
  long: (n.body ?? '').split('\n').length > 10 || (n.body ?? '').length > 700,
}));

// Where a dump can be filed. Drafts first — a draft is what you are actively
// writing, so it is the likely destination — then published, newest first
// within each.
//
// SERVER-RENDERED AND FILTERED IN THE BROWSER, the same call LinkSheet makes
// and for the same reason: there are 60 of these, it is already one round trip,
// and a keystroke that has to ask the server is slower and no more correct.
const { data: pieceRows } = await supabase
  .from('fragments')
  .select('id, title, slug, status, updated_at')
  .eq('type', 'writing')
  .neq('status', 'note')
  .is('deleted_at', null)
  .order('status', { ascending: true }) // draft < published
  .order('updated_at', { ascending: false });

const pieces = (pieceRows ?? []).map((p) => ({
  id: p.id,
  title: p.title?.trim() || '(untitled)',
  status: p.status,
}));

/*
  TRIAGE'S TWO HQ-WARD DESTINATIONS (14 · Piece 2). Both sheets are the real
  ones, opened prefilled — 10-hq §11's whole reason for saying triage needed no
  lab of its own was that it "rides sheets that already exist".

  ⚠ THE ROSTER DECIDES WHETHER LOGGING IS OFFERED AT ALL. An entry needs a
  person; with nobody on the roster the control would open a picker with
  nothing in it. So an empty roster renders no row and no sheet, rather than a
  disabled one beside a sentence explaining why (10-hq §10b, §10i).

  Active goals only, for the same reason the tasks room gives: filing a task
  under something you paused is how a goal quietly becomes a folder again.
*/
const tz = await homeTimezone(supabase);
const todayYmd = localToday(tz);

const [{ data: goalData }, { data: rosterData }] = await Promise.all([
  supabase.from('goals').select('id, name').eq('status', 'active').order('created_at'),
  supabase.from('people').select('id, display_name, photo_path').is('archived_at', null).order('display_name'),
]);
const goals = goalData ?? [];
const roster = rosterData ?? [];
---

<AdminLayout title="Notes" active="notes">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-8">
    <PageHeader title="Notes" />

    {
      notes.length === 0 ? (
        /* A room with nothing in it says one plain sentence and offers the way
           in — never an empty skeleton of the cards that would be here
           (10-hq §10b). */
        <p class="text-base-content/55 font-sans text-sm">
          Nothing here yet. The <span class="text-base-content/80">✚</span> in the corner is the way in — from any room.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <ul id="notes-pile" class="space-y-3">
          {notes.map((n) => (
            <li class="dump" data-note={n.id} data-slug={n.slug} data-updated={n.updatedAt}>
              {/* The words as they mean, not as they are spelled. Line breaks
                  survive (`breaks: true` above), so a dump's shape is still
                  part of what it says — bold is now bold as well. */}
              <div
                class:list={['dump__text', 'reading', 'jot-prose', n.long && 'dump__text--clamped']}
                data-text
                set:html={n.html}
              />
              {n.long && (
                <button type="button" class="dump__more" data-more>
                  more
                </button>
              )}
              {/* ⚠ THE CARD'S MARKDOWN, AND IT NEVER SHOWS. The editor is ONE
                  element shared by the whole pile (below), so a card cannot
                  hold its own — but four motions need this card's text without
                  one: the chooser's four destinations read it, and the save
                  sends it. Hidden rather than a `data-` attribute because a
                  jotting is multi-line and a textarea is the element that
                  carries newlines without ceremony. */}
              <textarea hidden data-edit-box set:text={n.body} />

              {/* THREE CONTROLS, NOT SIX (14 · Piece 2). Piece 1 shipped four —
                  edit, two ways out, delete — and Piece 2 adds two more ways
                  out. Six grey glyphs under a card that might hold three words
                  is the "many elements competing for attention" smell, and the
                  four destinations are not four questions anyway: they are one
                  question, asked once. So reading (✎) and discarding (🗑) stay
                  direct, and → opens the chooser. */}
              <div class="dump__foot">
                <time class="dump__stamp" datetime={n.updatedAt} data-ago>
                  {n.ago}
                </time>
                <button type="button" class="dump__act" data-edit title="Edit" aria-label="Edit">
                  <Icon name="ph:pencil-simple" />
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="dump__act" data-file title="Turn it into…" aria-label="Turn it into">
                  <Icon name="ph:arrow-right" />
                </button>
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="dump__act dump__act--danger"
                  data-delete
                  title="Delete"
                  aria-label="Delete"
                >
                  <Icon name="ph:trash" />
                </button>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* The chooser — ONE menu for the whole pile, positioned against whichever
      card's → opened it (scripts/pop-anchor.ts).

      ⚠ A TOP-LAYER POPOVER, and it has to be: trap 7. A menu anchored inside a
      card would be clipped or covered, and it would be inert in a way typecheck,
      build and a screenshot all pass. `popover="auto"` also hands Escape,
      light-dismiss and one-open-at-a-time to the browser.

      THE ORDER IS FREQUENCY, NOT SYMMETRY. A dump is far more often an errand
      than an essay seed, so the task is first — the same rule the interaction
      kinds follow (`KINDS` in lib/hq/interactions.ts). */
  }
  <div class="pop pop--wide" popover id="dump-file" data-pop="file">
    {
      /* ⚠ ONE ROW, NOT TWO — and it does not say "task", because it does not
        always make one. Michael, 2026-08-04: *"I don't want the cognitive
        expenditure of thinking: is this more of an event or more of a task?"*
        The reading decides, the sheet that opens says which and why, and the
        other shape is one tap away. Both live in the Agenda room, which is
        what this row can honestly promise. */
    }
    <button type="button" class="pop__row" data-as="agenda">
      <Icon name="ph:calendar-blank" /> Add to the Agenda…
    </button>
    {
      /* ⚠ ABSENT ENTIRELY WHEN THERE IS NOBODY TO LOG ABOUT (10-hq §10b, and the
        rule TaskSheet applies to its goal picker). An entry needs a person, so
        with an empty roster this row could only ever open a picker with nothing
        in it — a control that asks a question with no answers. */
    }
    {
      roster.length > 0 && (
        <button type="button" class="pop__row" data-as="log">
          <Icon name="ph:note-pencil" /> Log an entry…
        </button>
      )
    }
    <button type="button" class="pop__row" data-as="piece">
      <Icon name="ph:pen-nib" /> Make a piece
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="pop__row" data-as="append">
      <Icon name="ph:article" /> Add to a piece…
    </button>
  </div>

  {
    /* ONE PICKER, MANY CARDS — the TaskSheet rule. The room lists dozens of
      dumps and 60 destinations; rendering a dialog per card would be 60 lists
      in the DOM to keep in step. The card being filed is written onto the
      dialog when it opens. */
  }
  <dialog id="note-file" class="modal-dialog w-full max-w-md" aria-labelledby="note-file-title">
    <div class="flex max-h-[80vh] flex-col overflow-hidden font-sans">
      <header class="border-base-300 flex shrink-0 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b px-5 py-3.5">
        <h2 id="note-file-title" class="font-display text-lg">Add to a piece</h2>
        <button type="button" data-close class="text-base-content/50 hover:text-base-content" aria-label="Close"
          >✕</button
        >
      </header>

      <div class="shrink-0 px-5 py-3">
        <input
          id="note-file-q"
          type="search"
          class="input input-sm input-bordered w-full"
          placeholder="Search pieces…"
          aria-label="Search pieces"
        />
      </div>

      <ul id="note-file-list" class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-2 pb-3">
        {
          pieces.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="rounded-field hover:bg-base-200 flex w-full items-baseline gap-2 px-3 py-2 text-left text-sm"
                data-piece={p.id}
                data-title={p.title}
              >
                <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate">{p.title}</span>
                {/* A draft and a published piece are different places to put a
                  loose paragraph, and the chip is the whole explanation. */}
                {p.status === 'draft' && <span class="chip chip--off shrink-0">draft</span>}
              </button>
            </li>
          ))
        }
        {pieces.length === 0 && <li class="text-base-content/55 px-3 py-2 text-sm">No writing yet.</li>}
      </ul>
      <p id="note-file-none" class="text-base-content/55 px-5 pb-4 text-sm" hidden>Nothing matches.</p>
    </div>
  </dialog>

  {
    /* The real sheets, opened prefilled — not smaller copies of them. A task
      made from a dump gets the same date, effort, lead and recurrence controls
      as one typed in the Agenda room, because it is the same form. */
  }
  <TaskSheet today={todayYmd} goals={goals} />
  {
    /* The event sheet rides along for the half of readings that come back
      event-shaped. It takes the roster so a dinner can be tagged with who was
      there in the same motion — the one thing an event does that a task
      cannot, and the thing the drift guard reads (10-hq §10g). */
  }
  <EventSheet today={todayYmd} people={roster} />
  {roster.length > 0 && <LogSheet roster={roster} today={todayYmd} />}

  {
    /* The way back from a one-tap action. Lives outside the list so a card
      leaving the pile cannot take its own undo with it. */
  }
  <div id="notes-undo" class="undo-bar" role="status" aria-live="polite">
    <span data-undo-text></span>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-xs font-sans" data-undo-do>Undo</button>
  </div>

  {
    /* ONE EDITOR FOR THE WHOLE PILE — the same rule the chooser and the piece
      picker already follow in this room, and here it is not just tidiness. The
      pencil now opens a full TipTap editor (2026-08-06, Michael: the writing
      sheet's formatting is wanted here too, *"even though they take up a
      little bit of UI space"*), and a room that lists a hundred jottings
      cannot mount a hundred of those. So this shell is MOVED into whichever
      card is being edited and moved back out again, and its home is here —
      outside `#notes-pile`, so a card leaving the pile can never take the
      room's only editor with it.

      ⚠ THE TOOLBAR SITS BELOW THE SURFACE, not above it. Entering edit mode
      must not make the words move — that is the one thing that would make
      in-place editing feel like an "open" after all — so the editor takes the
      text's place at the text's metrics and the controls appear underneath,
      between the words and the card's own foot. */
  }
  <div id="dump-shell" class="dump__shell" hidden>
    <div id="dump-editor"></div>
    <EditorToolbar class="dump__tools" image />
  </div>
  <LinkDialog id="dump-link-dialog" />
  <AltTextDialog id="dump-alt-dialog" />

  <script>
    import '../../scripts/notes';
  </script>
</AdminLayout>
